    "id": "wp_159",
    "title": "AMBITION AND THE KINGDOM; CAN THEY CO-EXIST?",
    "slug": "ambition-and-the-kingdom-can-they-co-exist",
    "coverImageUrl": "https://thepublisherspen.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/whatsapp-image-2026-06-29-at-9.57.31-am.jpeg?w=1024",
    "excerpt": "There is a way to look at a man’s life and call him ambitious. Picture this. A young man in the ancient Middle East, somewhere around Turkey, living under his father’s roof. Not temporarily or as a phase – this is home. This is the structur",
    "body": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">There is a way to look at a man’s life and call him ambitious. Picture this.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify wp-block-paragraph\">A young man in the ancient Middle East, somewhere around Turkey, living under his father’s roof. Not temporarily or as a phase &#8211; this is home. This is the structure of how life works. His father is established and rooted in a family inheritance passed down through generations. Everything about the household is ordered and predictable. The kind of environment where your future is not something you build from scratch. He is not just married, but married to a woman people notice when she walks into a space. A beautiful and composed woman, the kind whose presence turns heads without trying; the desire of most bachelors. This is the kind of man you’d say already has “his life together” a wife, a home, a lineage in wealth and stability.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify wp-block-paragraph\">For most people that’s the goal, to be settled, secure and free. Yet one day this man wakes up and walks away from all of it. Not because something went wrong, not because he failed or was forced out, but because he had a new goal. His desire was stirred up for something different from the luxury, comfort, fame, autonomy, wealth and belonging he had. This desire directed his decision to leave, and channeled his effort towards this new goal. I would have thought that ambition is a respecter of class and status.</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group\"><div class=\"wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify wp-block-paragraph\">As humans, we have the innate ability to desire things built into our biological, physiological and social design irrespective of the era we were born, race, status, culture or religion. In a world that values structure, predictability, and visible progress, taking uncertain and risky journeys in spite of invisible proofs of attainment doesn&#8217;t read as wisdom, it reads as ambition. Sometimes qualified as vague. When Christians discuss ambition, they are often discussing a wide range of pursuits: career advancement, influence, leadership, ministry growth, achievement, wealth creation, purpose, and personal goals. Before asking whether any of these things are compatible with the Kingdom of God, we must first ask a more fundamental question; what exactly is ambition?</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ambition is a strong desire to achieve, attain, become, or accomplish something, coupled with a willingness to pursue it through sustained effort. In simple terms ambition is the pursuit of a desired future. If you have followed this picture closely, you would conclude that this man is being moved by something powerful. He knows where he is going, he is willing to leave comfort behind. His decisions are being shaped by a future that others cannot yet see. By every visible measure, he appears ambitious. Yet that conclusion while understandable, is incomplete.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify wp-block-paragraph\">When Abraham left Padan-Aram looking for a city whose builder and maker is God, his journey was not driven by a future he imagined for himself, he was not acting upon a personal vision board, he was responding to a divine invitation. Ambition, as we commonly encounter it, often begins with the self. The self identifies a lack, imagines a future, and mobilizes resources to bring that future into reality. But Abraham&#8217;s pursuit did not begin with self-discovery, it began with divine instruction. The direction of his life was not self-authored. It was God-initiated and this right here raises the confusion in &#8220;Christian discussions” because scriptures rarely condemn ambition in general. If ambition is totally outlawed in Christendom, what do we call a life that carries all the visible traits of ambition (drive, endurance, zeal, perseverance, focus, sacrifice) but is not sourced from the self? Is it still ambition? Or is it something else entirely?</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify wp-block-paragraph\">Once ambition is reduced to effort, then every disciplined person is ambitious. If it is reduced to desire, then every longing becomes ambition. But ambition is not merely the presence of desire or drive, it is the direction of that desire and drive. It is not just that you are driven. It is what your drive is anchored on. There is a kind of ambition that begins with the self and ends with the self, scripture calls this selfish ambition.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify wp-block-paragraph\">James 3:14-16 ESV &#8220;<em>But if you have bitter jealousy and selfish ambition in your hearts, do not boast and be false to the truth. This is not the wisdom that comes down from above, but is earthly, unspiritual, demonic. For where jealousy and selfish ambition exist, there will be disorder and every vile practice and it is consistently warned against&#8221;.</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify wp-block-paragraph\">But there is another kind of life, a life where desire is implanted, direction is given by God, and pursuit is sustained by God Himself, and the outcome is for his glory. From the outside, both pursuits may look intense, both may involve sacrifice, both may look driven, both may demand faithfulness over long periods of time, but one is self-authored while the other is God-authored. The source of one&#8217;s ambition is what qualifies it as good or bad; that&#8217;s why scripture sternly warns against self-ambition, self-authored. If Abraham had described his pursuit to the people of his time, they might have qualified his ambition as selfish because they were unaware of its source, but heaven reveals the fuller story. He was not chasing a future he invented; self- authored, he was responding to a call he received; God-authored.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify wp-block-paragraph\">As long as you remain on the face of the earth as a human, you will feel desire, you will experience drive, you will find within yourself the capacity to strive, build, pursue, lead and create. The kingdom, does not remove this capacity, rather it examines its source; because in the kingdom of God, ambition is not validated by intensity. It is validated by source. Not every powerful desire originates from God, not every compelling vision is God-given, not every pursuit that appears noble is heaven-inspired.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\"><em>&#8220;Unless the Lord builds the house those who build it labor in vain.&#8221; (Psalm 127:1)</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-justify wp-block-paragraph\">The answer to the question if ambition can coexist with the kingdom is clear. Perhaps the deeper question is this: When we examine the desires shaping our lives, are we pursuing futures we have authored for ourselves, or are we responding to futures God has authored for us? For Abraham, what looked like ambition from the outside was ultimately obedience expressed through pursuit.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Written by Idongesit Etokimo</p>\n</div></div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>",
