    "id": "wp_137",
    "title": "Set Apart; What Biblical Holiness Demands",
    "slug": "set-apart-what-biblical-holiness-demands",
    "coverImageUrl": "https://thepublisherspen.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/set-apart.png?w=724",
    "excerpt": "We live in a world where the call to be “set apart” is gradually losing its true meaning. With every shift in culture, there seems to be a steady decline in its clarity and viability. Time and again, a revised version is packaged and presen",
    "body": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We live in a world where the call to be “set apart” is gradually losing its true meaning. With every shift in culture, there seems to be a steady decline in its clarity and viability. Time and again, a revised version is packaged and presented to us in the form of public morality, managed by performance, measured by appearance, and shaped by individual or inherited beliefs that attempt to blend the language of God with culture.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While culture seeks to enforce this blend, though both cannot truly coexist; God says, “Come out from among them and be separate” (2 Corinthians 6:17).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">But what does it mean to be set apart?</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In Scripture, the Hebrew word qadosh means sacred, distinct, and consecrated to God. It is often used to describe the nature and character of God Himself, implying a relationship of ownership between God and the one He sets apart. This is clearly expressed in Leviticus 20:26 (CSB). This reveals God’s stance on holiness: it was never meant to be an occasional practice, but a divine demand for consecrated living.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Man was created in the image and likeness of God. The demand for holiness is rooted in this design; that man would embody the nature and character of God, which is holiness. Therefore, we are called to be holy just as He is holy. This is not merely a moral imitation; it is both a command and an invitation into an already established and existent life—one from which the believer draws identity and direction. Before holiness is ever visible in conduct, it is established in covenant. God claims a people, and that claim carries consequences for how they live.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If holiness means being set apart, then what are we set apart from, and unto whom?</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To be set apart is to belong wholly to God—to be drawn out from what is common and given over to Him. It is a life marked by difference, not for the sake of appearance, but as evidence of ownership. Your life is no longer governed by personal desires, but by the will of the One who has called you. When God sets a man apart, He begins to shape his life with intention. There are boundaries that begin to form—not as restrictions meant to harm, but as guidance meant to preserve purpose.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We see this in Samson, who was set apart even before birth. His life carried specific instructions that defined his calling. We also see it in John the Baptist, whose lifestyle, choices, and even expression stood in quiet contrast to the world around him.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Their lives remind us that consecration is not always comfortable, but it is always purposeful. To be set apart is to embrace a life of obedience. A non-negotiable posture, consistent and regulated by God.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Living this life will cost you.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It may cost you relationships. Not everyone will understand the version of you that chooses obedience over compromise. Some people are only familiar with who you were before you began to walk closely with God, and your growth may create distance where there was once ease.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It may cost you opportunities. There will be open doors you must walk away from, not because they are obviously wrong, but because they are not aligned with who you are becoming. It will confront your desires. The things you once entertained freely: what you watch, what you listen to, what you give your attention to, will begin to change. There will be a call to restraint where there was once indulgence.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It may cost you comfort. You will find yourself in spaces where you cannot fully participate, in conversations you cannot engage in, and in environments that no longer feel like home. At times, this may feel like isolation, but it is often the evidence of transformation. And perhaps most importantly, it will cost you control. There is a surrender that comes with being set apart; laying down of your own plans and your preferences. You begin to trust God not just with parts of your life, but with the whole of it. You become a passenger in your own vehicle.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">All of these exemplifies the need and importance of consecration in becoming holy. Which is not inherently sinful but they often reveal the weights we must lay aside as we journey ahead which may differ from person to person.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Holiness, then, is not an accessory that complements the Christian life; it is a vital necessity, it is what makes the life of the believer different and in turn acceptable for use before God. The call to consecration was never meant to strip away one’s will or desires. Rather, through a new and living way, it reshapes them into a renewed sense of priority. You begin to prefer God’s will over simply “following your heart”.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In the early stages of this transformation, there may be friction, a tension between surrender and conformity to this new nature. This is because consecration does not happen by human strength, but by the strength of the One who supplies it. As we yield in total surrender, grace is given, grace that is unique to each individual. God has not called us to a version of holiness that we can comfortably sustain on our own. He calls us into a life fully devoted and dependent on Him, where His will becomes the priority and no longer subject to our desires. This is what it means to live by every word (Matthew 4:4).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Consecration is never accidental, nor does it thrive in disorder. No one drifts into loving God, and no one grows by chance. While holiness is a command, it also requires intentional pursuit (Hebrews 12:14 NLT). It is a private reality long before it becomes a public expression. Holiness is not reserved for a select group of pastors, evangelists, or ministers. It is the calling of every believer. If God commands it, then grace is available to walk in it, as long as there is a willing heart.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is not a call to performative difference, but to genuine transformation. A difference should be evident, not staged, but formed, as we are shaped from all we are not into all that He desires us to be.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is the life we were created for: a life obediently yielded, and wholly submitted to Him. To be holy or die trying!</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>",
