    "id": "wp_129",
    "title": "GOD OR THE IDEA OF GOD: THE DANGERS OF ROMANTICIZING YOUR RELATIONSHIP WITH GOD",
    "slug": "god-or-the-idea-of-god-the-dangers-of-romanticizing-your-relationship-with-god",
    "coverImageUrl": "https://thepublisherspen.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/whatsapp-image-2026-03-13-at-9.40.33-am.jpeg?w=819",
    "excerpt": "To romanticise is to idealise, to reshape something into what we prefer rather than what it truly is. It is a subtle distortion, elevating what is pleasant but ignoring what confronts. We live in a time when God is often viewed through a mi",
    "body": "<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">To romanticise is to idealise, to reshape something into what we prefer rather than what it truly is. It is a subtle distortion, elevating what is pleasant but ignoring what confronts.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">We live in a time when God is often viewed through a mirror of preference rather than revelation. “If I do not feel him, then it means he is not here”. If he does not affirm us, we reinterpret Him. Slowly God becomes a reflection of our emotional state rather than the sovereign Lord of Scripture.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">God is reduced to a curated concept that is shaped by emotional needs; trauma avoidance, desire for affirmation, personal ambition and other self soothing vices instead of encountered as He reveals Himself. Yet the relationship God calls us into is not built on feelings but obedience. “If you love Me, keep My commandments” (John 14:15). With God, the foundation of intimacy is obedience, something romanticism resists by nature. Emotions are not inherently bad in themselves, even Jesus had emotions. He got angry, he felt compassion for the people, he even wept: &#8220;Jesus wept&#8221; (John 11:35). But, beneath the expression of his feelings, was a heart sold out to the precepts of God in obedience and perfect submission.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">Romanticizing your relationship with God distorts your view of Him and removes you from the guardrails of Scripture. It creates an invented partial god. A god who always agrees with your path, preferences, and supports your ambitions. A god who never says no. A god who never interrupts your plans. A god who makes no costly demands. A god without authority. A god without government — a god who does not exist.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">The nature of God defines love as obedience but the idea of God redefines love as emotions and vibes, creating an illusion of intimacy while preserving autonomy.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">It is what Saul did.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">In 1 Samuel 15, Saul was commanded by God to completely destroy the Amalekites. Instead he spared King Agag and preserved the best of the livestock. Saul&#8217;s reasoning sounded spiritual, he claimed the animals were saved for sacrifice. But beneath the justification was self-preservation,. He feared the people and protected his image before them. He partially obeyed while appearing fully devoted, and when confronted, he insisted he had obeyed the Lord.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">That is romanticized obedience, devotion that edits the command to protect the self. Saul’s act of half obedience cost him his throne and a possible hope of a lineage of kingship, all because he placed reputation above surrender.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">Romanticizing your relationship with God is the most sophisticated form of self-preservation. It allows someone to feel spiritual and devoted while retaining control. The danger is subtle and severe.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">It goes on to produce spiritual stagnation disguised as intimacy. No real transformation takes place, there is a false sense of assurance and growth is stunted because emotions cannot sustain covenant faithfulness.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">You begin to create a god in your own image. Idolatry is not only bowing to statues; it is reducing God to a manageable concept where obedience is replaced with emotion.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">You become resistant to correction. “The Lord disciplines the one He loves” (Hebrews 12:6). When intimacy with God is defined as comfort, discipline feels like rejection. You interpret conviction as condemnation and avoid growth. When intimacy is defined by comfort, discipline feels like rejection.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">Romanticizing your walk with God trains you to expect comfort, making you suppress conviction over time. You slowly desensitize your conscience while believing you are spiritually healthy. “Do not grieve the Holy Spirit” (Ephesians 4:30).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">You follow Jesus emotionally but not sacrificially. It becomes an affair of convenience and comfort rather than obedience and surrender in opposition to what Jesus said, “If anyone would come after Me, let him deny himself and take up his cross daily” (Luke 9:23).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">You remain spiritually immature. Rollng in a coaster of emotional circles without depth, you experience intensity without changing.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">You cultivate affection without reverence for God, and affection without awe births familiarity. “The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom” (Proverbs 9:10).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">And here is the question that must be faced:</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">When last did obedience to God cost you something you wanted to keep?</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">The ultimate danger is self-deception. You can believe you belong to God while never truly submitting to Him. You can feel deeply connected to Him while still never fully surrendering to him, yet Scripture warns that not everyone who says “Lord, Lord” will enter the kingdom (Matthew 7:21–23).</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\">In the end, you will not stand before the God you imagined but the God who is, the One who required your life and everything in between. The tragedy is not open rebellion, it is standing before a holy God and discovering that what you called intimacy was only comfort, and what you called surrender was a life controlled by the convenience of self.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left wp-block-paragraph\"></p>",
    "author": "Pastor Ngbede Odeh",
    "categories": [
      "Faith & Doctrine",
      "Spiritual Maturity"
    ],
